Give the evaluator a readable statistics report, and a setting for counting.

nanopynix embeds the evaluator, and it needs two things that upstream Nix
does not give an embedded caller.

**A report that a caller can read.** `EvalState::printStatistics` builds the
report and then writes it, to stderr or to the file that `NIX_SHOW_STATS_PATH`
names. A library caller can use neither. This patch splits the function: a new
`statisticsJSON` builds the report and returns it, and `printStatistics` keeps
its behavior and calls the new function. No output changes.

**A setting, rather than an environment variable.** `countCalls` comes from
`getEnv("NIX_COUNT_CALLS")` at the moment the evaluator is constructed, so the
choice belongs to the whole process. nanopynix gives each evaluator its own
settings, and it builds an evaluator while other evaluators run, so `setenv`
from one thread is a data race. `count-calls` is now an ordinary
`EvalSettings` option. The environment variable still turns counting on, so a
program that already uses it keeps working.

**Upstream carries the third part now.** This patch also made the three
call-count maps concurrent, because nanopynix gives each evaluator its own
thread and two threads then write one map. Upstream did the same in
`f7d25a76a` ("Make NIX_COUNT_CALLS thread-safe") and `542bf53af` ("Use
cvisit_all"), with the same `boost::concurrent_flat_map` behind a `ref` and
the same `try_emplace_or_visit`. The upstream defect this patch used to
correct beside it -- `auto list = topObj["attributes"]` copying the value out
of the document, so every attribute count was built and discarded -- is
corrected there too. All of it is gone from here.

`nix-2.34-count-calls.patch` and `nix-2.35-count-calls.patch` are the older,
larger change for the two pinned versions, which predate those commits.

This file is selected for any version with no entry of its own, which today
means nix git alone. It applies at or after `f7d25a76a`. A released 2.36 will
predate that commit, so it needs a file of its own rather than this one.

